+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Memory Constraints:** Naive differentiable rendering of high-resolution images is intractable due to memory limits [S1]. The patch-based approach with stride sampling is required [S1].
+*   **Mode Collapse:** The absolute directional CLIP loss can cause mode collapse of the generator and hurt generation diversity [S2].
+*   **Insufficient Magnitude:** Directional loss alone struggles to reach sufficient stylization magnitude, resulting in weak style transfer [S2]. Global contrastive losses may be needed to ensure proper magnitude [S2].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is limited to 500 iterations [S1]. The use of patch-based rendering ($128 \times 128$) and reduced sampling (16 importance samples) significantly reduces memory usage compared to full-resolution rendering, allowing optimization on standard hardware [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory constraints are the primary bottleneck for high-resolution rendering; the patch method mitigates this [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables text-driven stylization of 3D scenes; relative loss improves diversity and avoids mode collapse [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** May suffer from insufficient stylization magnitude if only directional loss is used [S2]. Performance depends heavily on the CLIP model's ability to capture semantic features [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When text-guided artistic stylization of a NeRF is required [S1, S2].
-*   **When not to use:** When photometric accuracy is the primary goal (use standard NeRF training) or when high-fidelity magnitude transfer is critical without additional contrastive mechanisms [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Determined by 500 iterations of patch rendering and CLIP encoding [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced by using $128 \times 128$ patches and 16 importance samples per ray instead of full-resolution rendering [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables text-guided stylization of NeRFs; efficient memory usage via patch rendering [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Absolute directional loss risks mode collapse and insufficient stylization magnitude [S2].
+*   **When to use:** Text-driven NeRF stylization where memory is constrained [S1, S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When high diversity or strong magnitude stylization is required without additional contrastive losses [S2].
 
 ## Background
-CLIPNeRF was introduced by Wang et al. in 2021, extending the StyleCLIP and StyleGAN-NADA frameworks to 3D neural radiance fields [S2]. It was designed to enable text-driven editing and stylization of 3D scenes by leveraging the semantic alignment capabilities of the CLIP model [S1, S2]. The method builds on the observation that global directional losses in CLIP space can guide stylization but may lead to mode collapse, prompting the development of relative directional losses [S2].
+CLIPNeRF was introduced by Wang et al. in 2021, extending the StyleCLIP and StyleGAN-NADA frameworks to 3D radiance fields [S2]. It was designed to enable text-driven editing of NeRFs by aligning rendered views with text prompts in the CLIP embedding space [S2]. The method addresses the intractability of full-resolution differentiable rendering by using efficient patch-based sampling [S1].
 
